body:
@KoBeWi fair point. I just tried with an empty Godot project, and it works with 4.7.dev4, but it fails when there is GDExtension registered `EditorDebuggerPlugin` 🤔 

So, after a quick check in the empty project, I see that if an `EditorPlugin` adds an editor debugger plugin but does not remove it, the editor crashes. This was not required previously, although it's probably good practice to do it.

But unregistration is not required for inspector plugins, export plugins, etc, so I would say that the editor crashing because the debugger plugin isn't removed is at least inconsistent.

Ok, your plugin's code is invalid, though you are aware of that based on the comments above.
```c++
void MrpEditorPlugin::_exit_tree() {
    // remove_debugger_plugin(_debugger_plugin);
    _debugger_plugin.unref();

    //remove_inspector_plugin(_inspector_plugin);
    _inspector_plugin.unref();
}
```
This is obviously wrong, you can't free a plugin without removing it. The crash occurs because the EditorDebuggerNode has an invalid pointer in its plugin HashSet and it crashes when it tries to clear session list. The reason it doesn't crash in case of inspector plugin is likely because it does literally nothing. It doesn't have any fields to cleanup (unlike debugger plugin, which is more complex), so the invalid plugin manages to slip by without memory violation.

The "regression" here is that the debugger node is now cleaned up later. Previously it happened when it was exiting tree, so before any user plugin had a chance to mishandle pointers.
